[Music]  thank you very much for joining us today  you just released your book feminism for  women challenging the direction that  modern feminism is taking would you mind  telling us a bit about the book and what  inspired you to write it  i've wanted to write this book for a  long time because  i  encounter constantly younger generations  of feminists so i'm 59 so there might be  a whole two generations younger than me  saying that for them things are really  difficult right now  because of the proliferation of really  extreme sadistic pornography that seems  to be just  normalized amongst young men even boys  and the effect that that has on them  but also the way that  young feminists  are trying to challenge orthodoxies  such as for example the notion that sex  work is work  when it is understood by feminists  globally who campaign against male  violence as abuse and exploitation of  the most disenfranchised women so  indigenous women poor women women of  color black women  all of those that are targeted  disproportionately  compared to other populations  to be abused by men  so they are being told by often by male  leaders self-proclaimed leaders  of feminist societies of lgbt groups  in university settings and elsewhere on  social media and in real life  that they are wrong that they are prudes  that they are anti-sex  that they need to lighten up  that in fact sex work is liberating  empowering a woman's choice  and it was after encountering  women telling me these things  for a number of years  that i decided to write a book that  might be helpful for them  whether they agree with it or not is  really beside the point but it might  help them understand  how we got to a situation  where what passes for feminism today  seems to benefit men more than it does  women so how do you describe the  difference between liberation feminism  and equality feminism and what societal  movements triggered the shift from one  to the other do you think the feminism  that i became involved in at the very  end of the 1970s when i was still in my  teens i was 17 years old  was  anti-male violence campaigning  so i met with feminists that were older  than me  in leeds a city in the north of england  where there was a big manhunt  for a serial killer called peter  sutcliffe  who  had killed women sadistically brutally  um  killed several women who the police had  decided were involved in prostitution so  therefore they didn't matter as much as  other women um and it was this bungled  police  operation  where women were told to stay at home at  night  but men were not  and whether  the press the national and local press  reflected that attitude of women  not really mattering if they'd been  prostituted or if they were somehow  behaving as women should not in other  words they were out drinking in pubs  they were on their own  that led me to  prioritizing this issue  because what i came to understand  was that women can never be liberated if  we are fearing and experiencing male  violence from flashing to sexual  harassment in the workplace or on street  through to rape and sexual assault and  femicide  the killing of women because we're women  and so that to me became much more  important  than what i call glass ceiling feminism  the kind of equality feminism that you  would see  in  some states in the us  in scandinavia  so take for example sweden norway  iceland those countries have really good  records when it comes to  equal pay pension rights maternity leave  maternity benefits child care et cetera  for women but the levels of rape  domestic violence and femicide are as  high  in countries where there is less social  and legal equality so what does that  mean and also in terms of political  representation  equality feminism would have us think  that  50 female members of parliament  will cancel out sexism  just imagine the chamber in the house of  commons  you've got 50 women and 50 men which  ones are going to speak the most  and the loudest and feel the most  entitled who's going to be heard more so  that's not going to work  you know give me 70 80 female mps we  might be talking  but then what about the violence and the  curtailment  of the lives of women in politics or  in  jobs  with zero hour contracts  it's the same in many ways if they're  experiencing coercive control sexual and  domestic violence  and their trauma is being  dismissed and they're being told that  they are to blame for what's happened to  them  so liberation feminism is to seek to end  all male violence  to see patriarchy and male dominance  as  the kind of deciding factor  in whether or not women can break out of  sexist  societies  and to recognize women as a sex class  and men as a sex class  and therefore in order to end male  violence and liberate women you have to  end male violence because it is the tool  that is used to keep women in our place  even though of course not all men  not all men commit acts of rape not all  men kill enough do  for it not to be necessary  to have a gun held at the head of every  woman it curtails our lives  so is it almost as if kind of liberation  feminism is what has to come first and  after that an equality feminism will  sort itself out almost well you know  equality feminism only benefits certain  women  who  have particular jobs  who are  not as poorly served as other women  so middle class upper middle class women  women from elite societies who are still  oppressed by sexism by patriarchy  still are targets for male violence  still are dismissed when they report  rape and sexual assault  but  seem to focus more  on things that will benefit them in  their middle class lives now they still  need feminism feminism is for every  woman not every woman is a feminist  obviously  but  equality  will not be an issue  after liberation because if we're  liberated from patriarchy  why would women be seen as lesser than  men  why would women  be  seen as undeserving disbelieved  we wouldn't be the target of  discrimination in that way  because there would be no need for that  constant reinforcement  of male  supremacy of male entitlement  so what role should men have in the  movement for a woman's liberation  there's a really crucial role for men  which is to  not just be a bystander  and to support feminism in our causes  and to do that it's not about wearing a  t-shirt saying hey i'm a great feminist  in fact i'm a better feminist than you  it's not asking for  a seat at the table  or  a speaking slot at a conference  to talk about the wonderful work that  you might be doing that men might be  doing  to end male violence it's actually about  recognizing  that it is inhumane  to abuse women it's inhumane to consume  the kind of pornography  where women are being tortured women are  being harmed women are being hurt women  are screaming begging  for her abuser to stop and for men to  get sexually excited by that that can  only happen with the dehumanization of  women  so the role of men is to say to other  men don't do it it's not cool  and let's talk about why  we have bought this line  that we are  going to be educated in  sex and relationships  through watching women being tortured  on set why have we been told that if we  fancy a bit of sex but it's not the kind  of sex that my girlfriend gives me or i  don't have a girlfriend  because i'm a bit of a jerk and i can't  get a real date that it's fine to go  into a brothel and pay for sexual access  to a woman who does not want you  and she's not consenting or the cash  wouldn't be involved to ask those very  difficult questions of other men  about making jokes about hitting women  about making jokes about ugly birds on  the night out  and constantly challenge  sexism  and talk to women  and hear about the experiences of women  when they're out at night in a taxi on  the way home after being drinking  or relaxing on holiday  and compare them to your own  because men don't have to go out  clutching their keys between their  fingers  some men are clearly targets  black men gay men you know of course  there can be targets of violence from  other men but not simply because they're  born male  with the new legislations around  abortion taking place in texas many  human rights organizations decide to use  the wording people who can get pregnant  rather than simply women to describe the  ones being affected by the new law would  you consider this a step towards  inclusivity or an act of erasure of  women it's not inclusive if you're  excluding  half of the planet  and i'd be perfectly happy never to use  gendered language again i've got no idea  what it feels like to be a woman  we don't know  there's nothing innate about it  it's only in relation to the way we're  treated by men and often our biology is  used as part of that  part of that punishment  so we're not oppressed because we have  biology  that is used to justify it  it's absolutely ridiculous to suggest  that before  women are liberated from patriarchy and  before we see an end to male violence  and male supremacy so we can pretend  that we're all people on an equal  playing field  so it's a pertinent question about the  abortion  issue  men do not need ever to access abortion  ever  and if trans men do that's because they  have female biology and that is a simple  biological fact that matters when we  look at  issues of health care  other medical issues  but what's really important is the  documentation  of  sex crimes  who's committing them and who against  whom  you have a situation as we currently do  with more than 20 police forces  choosing  to  log  a crime  that has been alleged against a man  where they choose to override  that person's sex biological sex with  gender identity what does that even mean  because rape is a sexed crime  only people with a penis can commit it  that's enshrined in our legislation  all of a sudden we're seeing reports in  newspapers and even ons reports  government statistics  that are skewing  the facts  where apparently all of a sudden  women are downloading  violent  child abuse imagery  women have been  arrested for rape  women have been arrested on child abuse  charges when in fact these are  biological males following the pattern  of male  behavior male behavior under patriarchy  not because it's innate  and women are not committing these  crimes  so that's why i think that until  the great revolution  we have to be very clear  about biological sex and we have to  record it as such people can define  however they want they can use whatever  pronouns  they can use whatever identity they wish  but we cannot pretend  in some orwellian twist  that  women  are raping when it's biological men and  in a dream scenario where do you see the  future of feminism heading and you know  similarly what do you fear well  feminism is the most optimistic movement  on the planet  we have to be  because we want to see change and we  work towards change the whole time  but also we don't believe that boys are  born  programmed to rape and to harm women and  we don't believe girls are born victims  so we recognize it's socialization  and it's the cultural context in which  boys grow  that give some permission  to act upon abusive fantasies and  scenarios  because it upholds power  so feminism  has a very bright future  because since  the beginning of the second wave of  feminism as it's known i just call it  feminism  more and more men have become educated  about their role  as human beings  that they cannot use the excuse that  they can't help themselves  that somehow they'll have to go out and  rape and kill women if they get sexually  frustrated or if they're disrespected in  some way  it's now understood even though some men  refuse to accept it  that none of this is innate or  programmed  and i see progress all over the world i  see legal and social and political  changes that have been brought about by  active feminists  what i fear is that young women will  become so silenced that they won't dare  practice real feminism  for fear of being censored silenced  punished  but  the more of us that can stand in front  of them  mentor and support them take the bullet  for them if need be  the more strength that they'll get  as  confident feminists and will move  forward in a very positive way  and why is government regulation  around you know  sites that clearly  put forward harmful ideas such as  pornography why is the the regulation so  lax  regulation on pornography in the sex  trade in general is lacks because it's  about profit  pornographers get very excited by profit  much more than they do about sex  and so  why would you regulate a hugely  profitable industry  it's as simple as that but the second  strand to this is that the victims are  women and girls primarily not all but  primarily  and we matter less than do men which is  why we've got feminism  you've written some really interesting  articles on um you know for example  umoja in  kenya a village where men are banned um  how have these experiences impacted your  personal feminist ideology and in  particular the value of women's only  spaces  i first met i was introduced to women  only spaces  as a very young feminist and as a young  lesbian that was really crucial because  of course i was under threat for being  in that lesbian  it was much harder than it is now but  it's not ideal now  so women only spaces were very important  to be able to talk to other women about  the danger of rejecting men and about  what we saw for ourselves for the future  doing something quite brave and quiet  out there  going to places like emoji  and seeing how women  who have  way less than we do in the west way less  in terms of resources  legislation to support them  really inspired me because every trip i  make where i go to  visit women who are resisting male  violence  but with the most resistance from their  governments and from men  makes me feel like i want to fight  harder  because if they can do it then so can we  but what it also does is it forges  solidarity between women across the  globe which is one of the most wonderful  things about the internet because now we  can meet each other without physically  doing the travelling  so doing the travelling is the best  thing ever  because i get to meet with them and i  get to learn so much from them and i get  to report on it  and my world has expanded  through that  and i feel extremely lucky to be able to  do it  julie bindu thank you very much  for more debates talks and interviews  subscribe today to the institute of art  and ideas at iai tv  you

